---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import HomePageLink from "../../components/HomePageLink.astro";
import ContactSection from "../../components/ContactSection.astro";
import RevealScript from "../../components/RevealScript.astro";
import speakingPhoto from "../../assets/about-speaking.png";
import {site} from "@/lib/site";
---

<Layout
  title="About — Becca Bailey"
  description="I'm a software engineer, engineering leader, writer, and researcher. I use data, stories, and reporting to understand communities and the people inside them."
>
  <div>
    <h1 class="mb-6 md:mb-8">About</h1>

    <section
      class="reveal mb-12 grid gap-8 md:mb-16 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_min(380px,38%)] md:items-start md:gap-10 lg:gap-12"
    >
      <div class="max-w-prose editorial-prose space-y-4 text-text-muted">
        <p class="text-text">
          I'm a software engineer, engineering leader, writer, and researcher.
          I use data, stories, and reporting to understand communities and the
          people inside them.
        </p>
        <p></p>
        <p>
          I often struggle with what to put here, because I don't have a linear
          career arc that fits into a short elevator pitch. I majored in Music
          Education, and spent the first few years after college graduation
          teaching music and art to elementary students in Chicago and
          Quetzaltenango, Guatemala. I had always been interested in web
          development, but I decided to turn it into a career in 2015. After a
          tech bootcamp and an apprenticeship, I became a front-end focused
          software engineer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since then, I have been an Engineering Manager, an open source
          maintainer for a widely-used data visualization tool, a design system
          engineer, and an accessibility advocate. Most recently, I had a short
          stint as a CTO for a legal tech startup, helping a founder turn an
          idea into a real product. I think people are the most important part
          of any tech product, and helping the people around me to understand
          technology is my passion and guiding principle.
        </p>
        <div class="pt-2">
          <HomePageLink href="/resume">My professional background</HomePageLink>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure
        class="m-0 w-full max-w-[380px] justify-self-center md:justify-self-end"
      >
        <Image
          src={speakingPhoto}
          alt="Becca Bailey speaking into a microphone on stage"
          class="image-rounded aspect-[3/4] w-full rounded-md object-cover shadow-soft"
          width={900}
          height={1200}
          loading="eager"
        />
      </figure>
    </section>

    <div class="max-w-prose">
      <section class="reveal mb-12">
        <h2 class="mb-6">Writing &amp; research</h2>
        <div class="editorial-prose space-y-4 text-text-muted">
          <p>
            When I first became a software engineer, a mentor told me that "all
            technical problems are people problems". Today, I think I would
            rephrase that to say that "all technical problems are systems
            problems". So, I write about systems problems, and the people who
            are affected by them. I want to help people in tech feel seen, and
            to help people outside of tech understand the systems that shape our
            work and our lives.
          </p>
          <p>
            An astonishing number of times, I found research or data about
            something I thought I was making up. That moment of recognition,
            “Oh. That's what I've been experiencing. I thought it was just me,”
            is what I want to create for readers of my newsletter,
            <a href={site.links.substack}>Performance Anxiety</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Having an engineering background means I can build tools to follow
            these questions, which is how I've ended up studying both <a
              href="/making-things/language-of-work"
              >the language on company careers pages</a
            > and <a href="/making-things/closing-window"
              >the odds of building an audience on Ravelry</a
            >. Sometimes I start with something smaller in
            <em>Did You See That?</em>, where I put something weird from work,
            tech, or culture in front of readers and ask them to help me figure
            out what we're looking at.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-start gap-4">
          <HomePageLink href="/writing">Read my writing</HomePageLink>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ContactSection class="mt-12 md:mt-16" />
  <RevealScript />
</Layout>
